It's not just like a slide.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.060
It's like, no, we're going to make you see it happen without being able to do anything

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:19.320
on your controller. So yeah, really brutal game.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:27.320
Yeah, so wait, you're saying that in the first one, this was all like in-game play, and then

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.740
in this one, it's cutscenes?

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.660
No, I was saying in the other versions of Double Dragon 2.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:36.480
Like in the NES version, they're nice enough to just like tell the story before you start

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.800
the game, and then when you start the game, you just go right into it.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:43.700
So I guess if you like are one of those people that skips the like start, you know, those

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:46.660
kinds of things, then like you just would just start the game and just start beating

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:50.700
people up and not knowing, you'd be oblivious to the idea that your girlfriend's dead.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:53.620
You just think like, yeah.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:57.320
I mean, I kind of like, so you're saying that the other version of this game don't have that

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:59.580
high fidelity art.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.240
No, yeah, instead you're right in the game, and they just make it happen in the game before

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:07.160
you can even, like, you know, you see your player there, but you can't do anything.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.840
Huh. I mean, I kind of like that.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:12.140
It means we kind of, we got more content that way.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:18.880
Yeah, I don't know though. We got, we got like a slideshow instead of, you know, I, I would opt for anything to happen.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:22.360
You know, come on, we're in like the God of War times where like those games are just

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:25.900
now they're all movies all the time, and there's never any cuts, right?

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:26.800
It's all seamless.

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:32.400
See, I think that the, the slideshow is more cinematic and epic.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:37.980
Like, but the, uh, I do get what you're saying about like the, the kind of harshness of like

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:42.860
you actually being there as the player, and it's like, well, nothing, you failed, you let

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:46.680
her die. Like, it kind of feels more, a little closer to home that way.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:50.600
Well, you cut the controls to my controller, so.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:51.900
No, no, no. Sounds like it's you.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.640
No, it was you. It was your fault, Sean.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:58.500
Okay. Sean, you're making me think of people just like pressing buttons, like trying to stop

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.440
it. Like this time it's going to be different.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:05.260
Yeah. I think there probably is one combination of inputs that you could do that is a cheat

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:06.500
code that allows you to save her.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:12.800
So. Ooh. Double Dragon 2, like the first game, is a side-scrolling beat-em-up where you attack enemies

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:19.220
on screen with punches and kicks and combinations of, uh, maybe jumping and kicks or punching

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:21.940
from behind or uppercuts or you get the idea.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:23.020
Fists and feet.

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:28.380
That's what you're using. This isn't the kind of game where like all of a sudden you have some magic abilities or something

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.540
like that. This is Fists of Fury going on here.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:35.840
And previously on Double Dragon, due to the technical limitations of the NES, uh, that

